<p>The Pentagon told lawmakers Tuesday that the U.S. war with Iran has cost about $29 billion so far, roughly $4 billion more than its $25 billion estimate late last month. Acting Pentagon comptroller Jules Hurst said the revised figure reflects updated repair and replacement costs for equipment, as well as operational expenses. Hurst testified alongside Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and Joint Chiefs Chairman Gen. Dan Caine during House and Senate budget hearings on the administration’s $1.5 trillion Pentagon request for 2027. Hegseth declined to say when the administration would send Congress a separate request to cover the Iran conflict, saying it would seek “whatever we think we need” outside the base Pentagon budget. Lawmakers from both parties pressed Hegseth on the administration’s goals in Iran, congressional authorization, the state of a ceasefire, and U.S. weapons stockpiles. Hegseth said reports of depleted munitions were inaccurate and told House lawmakers, “I take issue with the characterization that munitions are depleted in a public forum. That’s not true”.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Gqq22yea_rwd9n69z2ICH4nAHJgyWtwQKz5anS9W5KFpPfqJBJ8arwq6UqX5l1HJVjr0bWalbpweYgZUTuCR318QBDAetnLprIHZtlRNahlLMfisE_5_opSnbcni5c2FaASRoen4V7uF-vXxvNw5doGGlsnenZ5JyA8' alt='Pete Hegseth and Gen. Dan Caine attend a House Appropriations Subcommittee hearing on the FY2027 Defense Department budget request.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Midterm politics: Several accounts noted the hearings came about six months before the midterm elections, with Democrats trying to connect the war’s expense to cost-of-living frustration as Republicans face a difficult House fight.</li><li>Munitions bill: Pentagon comptroller Jay Hurst told lawmakers that about $24 billion of the total relates to replacing and repairing munitions, with the remainder also covering operational costs for deployed forces.</li><li>Economic pressure: The hearings coincided with U.S. inflation data showing April consumer inflation at 3.8% year over year, the highest since May 2023, with energy costs cited as a factor tied to the Iran conflict.</li><li>Hormuz operations: U.S. Central Command reported that 65 commercial ships had been redirected and four disabled under Washington’s naval blockade of Iran, while Iran broadened its operational definition of the Strait of Hormuz.</li></ul><blockquote>The joint staff team and the comptroller team are constantly looking at that estimate - Jules Hurst</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Pentagon comptroller Jules Hurst: Hurst described the $29 billion figure as a provisional Pentagon estimate that remains below outside projections. Lawmakers sought a fuller accounting. (<a href='https://www.politico.com/news/2026/05/12/pentagon-iran-war-money-00916656'>Politico</a>)</li><li>Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth: Hegseth faced pushback from both parties, including Republicans concerned about strains with traditional allies, while testifying on the administration’s Pentagon budget request. (<a href='https://www.nbcboston.com/news/national-international/hegseth-defends-budget-request-iran-war-cost/3948754/'>NBC Boston</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-iran-war-29-billion-dollars-in-60-days-iran-war-cost-16-percent-more-than-us-estimate-11487300#publisher=newsstand'>$29 Billion In 60 Days: Iran War Cost 16% More Than US Estimate</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1999806/iran-war-costs-soar-past-29bn-as-truce-teeters'>Iran war costs soar past $29bn as truce teeters</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/us-might-restart-striking-iran-trump-and-hegseth-warn'>US might restart striking Iran, Trump and Hegseth warn</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/05/13/pentagon-says-us-has-spent-29-billion-on-iran-war-so-far-amid-concerns-over-weapons-stockp'>Pentagon says US has spent $29 billion